From 74f860f157a0019180fcde0846dad56cf4bfa9ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:40:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 18/23] ad5x: x2600: Start-up tries that always reach the other
 slot

The SPL's second try could loop without ever trying the other slot: a
U-Boot proper that hung reset the attempt to 1 and set "U-Boot failed",
the next failed slot load sent the SPL back to U-Boot proper, which hung
again, and so on. A U-Boot proper started from a kernel slot also wrote
"U-Boot up", so the SPL loaded the same slot again.

Keep the whole plan in the CPM scratch pad instead (mach/ad5x_start.h):
the attempt, a persistent "skip U-Boot proper" bit, the slot and whether
it is forced, and the phase reached. Every value but a fresh one or
"kernel started" moves the attempt on; a U-Boot proper failure sets the
skip bit, a slot failure forces the other slot. U-Boot proper keeps the
fields and only changes the phase: "U-Boot up" when the SPL started it
from 0xA400, "U-Boot from a slot" when the SPL started it as a slot's
kernel (the SPL then boots the other slot), and as from slot B when
something else started it. So within four starts the board has started
a kernel or stops with the watchdog off.

The rules are plain inline C so that the laptop tests build them and walk
every failure combination through them.
